session:pr771Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #744 is the core wiring sub-issue of epic #741 (SPARQL SERVICE federation): actually issue the HTTP request to a SERVICE endpoint and feed results into evaluation, without making sparql_parser::execute/execute_with_base async (they run synchronously from the CLI, which has no tokio runtime, and from sparql_endpoint's async handlers, which hold the datastore under an async RwLock read guard while calling them). The real design choice was how to get pre-fetched results from the async handler into the synchronous executor without an async/sync impedance mismatch. Threading a new HashMap<ServiceKey, Vec<SolutionRow>> parameter through execute_with_base and every recursive eval_component arm (Optional/Union/Minus/Graph/Group/Subquery) would have touched ~8 existing call sites across the workspace (CLI, shacl, two test files, two sparql_endpoint handlers) for no benefit to any of them. Instead reused a pattern this exact file already established for an analogous problem (CURRENT_BASE/BaseGuard, issue #346): a thread-local holding pre-resolved SERVICE results, installed by a new ServiceResultsGuard RAII type around the synchronous execute_with_base call, consulted only inside the QueryComponent::Service evaluation arm via a stable (endpoint, serialized-body) ServiceKey computed identically on both the pre-resolution and lookup sides (issue #743's service_body_to_sparql_text). The guard is deliberately !Send (a PhantomData<*const ()> marker) so holding it across an .await point is a compile error, not a silently-broken multi-threaded-runtime bug. For the actual fetch: reused (not reimplemented) the SSRF-hardened client-building and capped-response-reading logic LOAD already uses (ssrf_preflight/is_blocked_ip, PRs #137/#138/#376), refactored into build_hardened_client/read_capped_response so SERVICE's POST and LOAD's GET share one hardening path; concurrency across multiple SERVICE endpoints in one query comes from tokio::task::spawn_blocking per endpoint plus awaiting all the JoinHandles, rather than the existing maybe_block_in_place helper (which only avoids blocking other tasks, not actual concurrency across several sequential calls within one task)." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:771 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr771Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr771 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr771Summary agp:decisionPoint session:pr771Decision .

session:pr771Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Deserialize each fetched SERVICE response into a throwaway, per-call scratch Datastore::new(64) rather than the real query datastore, and perform all async pre-resolution strictly before acquiring the real store's async RwLock read guard in sparql_endpoint's two query handlers." ;
    agp:alternative "from_sparql_json (#742) needs &mut Datastore only to intern RDF 1.2 triple-term and fresh-blank-node bindings -- the overwhelmingly common case (IRI/literal bindings) needs no mutable access at all. Restructuring the two query.rs handlers to briefly take a second, separate read (or a short write lock) on the real store just to deserialize federated JSON into it was considered and rejected for this issue: it complicates the lock-ordering story for a rare case (triple terms/blank nodes in federated SPARQL JSON results) and risks the dataset changing between two separate reads. The scratch-Datastore approach is documented as a known, deliberate limitation (a TripleTerm or blank-node binding from a federated result can carry IDs that only resolve correctly against that scratch store) and a follow-up issue (#772, sub-issue of #741) was filed for proper interning into the real datastore." .
